@* ================================================================ *@
@* DEMO STEP 1 (second half): finish the fallback route (CODE LIVE) *@
@*                                                                   *@
@* Note there is NO <NotFound> template in this router, and none is  *@
@* coming. In a Blazor Web App it compiles and never fires — not on  *@
@* the server pass, not on the interactive pass. Dead code, kept for *@
@* backward compatibility only. That is what makes it a trap.        *@
@*                                                                   *@
@* TODO: Add NotFoundPage="typeof(Pages.NotFound)" to <Router>       *@
@*       below. It does TWO jobs:                                    *@
@*                                                                   *@
@*   1. BAD URL (/nonexistent). The middleware from Step 1 fixes the *@
@*      SERVER pass. But the Blazor circuit then connects and re-runs *@
@*      this router against the URL the user actually typed, which    *@
@*      still matches nothing. NotFoundPage is what it renders. Leave *@
@*      it out and Blazor's bare "Not found" overwrites your styled   *@
@*      page a moment after it appears — the classic flash-then-gone. *@
@*                                                                   *@
@*   2. NavigationManager.NotFound() — a URL that DID match, but      *@
@*      whose data is missing (Step 3, ProductDetail.razor).          *@
@* ================================================================ *@
<Router AppAssembly="typeof(Program).Assembly">
    <Found Context="routeData">
        <RouteView RouteData="routeData" DefaultLayout="typeof(Layout.MainLayout)" />
        <FocusOnNavigate RouteData="routeData" Selector="h1" />
    </Found>
</Router>
